{
  "title": "Shopping at the market",
  "title_target": "Azokan erosketak egiten",
  "goal": "Shop using weights and measures, ask about prices, name colors, and compare two things.",
  "bekal": [
    "ask the price with *zenbat balio du?* and understand the answer",
    "use the units *kilo, kilo erdi, dozena, botila, zati*",
    "compare with *-ago*, *baino*, *-en*, and *-egi*"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Elkarrizketa — Erriberako azokan",
      "intro": "Saturday morning at the Ribera Market, Bilbo's biggest covered market, right on the riverbank.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Dendaria:",
          "text": "Egun on! Zer nahi duzu?",
          "id": "Good morning! What would you like?",
          "literal": "day good! what want you-have-it?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Sagarrak nahi ditut. Zenbat balio dute?",
          "id": "I'd like some apples. How much are they?",
          "literal": "apple-the(pl) want I-have-them. how-much worth they-have-it?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Dendaria:",
          "text": "Kiloa bi euro eta erdi.",
          "id": "Two and a half euros a kilo.",
          "literal": "kilo-the two euro and half."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Ondo, kilo bat mesedez. Eta tomateak?",
          "id": "OK, one kilo, please. And the tomatoes?",
          "literal": "fine, kilo one please. and tomato-the(pl)?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Dendaria:",
          "text": "Hiru euro kiloa. Gaur oso onak dira.",
          "id": "Three euros a kilo. They're very good today.",
          "literal": "three euro kilo-the. today very good-the(pl) are."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Kilo erdi nahi dut. Eta gazta hori zer da?",
          "id": "I'd like half a kilo. And what's that cheese?",
          "literal": "kilo half want I-have-it. and cheese that what is?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Dendaria:",
          "text": "Idiazabal gazta da, ardi esnearekin egina.",
          "id": "That's Idiazabal cheese, made from sheep's milk.",
          "literal": "Idiazabal cheese is, sheep milk-with made-the."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Garestia da?",
          "id": "Is it expensive?",
          "literal": "expensive-the is?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Dendaria:",
          "text": "Pixka bat garestiagoa, baina hemengo onena da.",
          "id": "A bit more expensive, but it's the best one here.",
          "literal": "little one expensive-more-the, but here-of good-most-the is."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Orduan zati txiki bat, mesedez.",
          "id": "Then a small piece, please.",
          "literal": "then piece small one, please."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Dendaria:",
          "text": "Hemen duzu dena: hamabi euro eta berrogeita hamar.",
          "id": "Here's everything: twelve euros fifty.",
          "literal": "here you-have-it all-the: twelve euro and fifty."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Hemen duzu. Eskerrik asko!",
          "id": "Here you are. Thank you!",
          "literal": "here you-have-it. thanks many!"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**Zenbat balio du? — the standard way to ask a price.** Literally \"how much is it worth?\". If the item is plural, the auxiliary goes plural too: *Zenbat balio **dute**?* Two equally common alternatives: *Zenbat da?* (shorter, more casual) and *Zein da prezioa?* (more formal).",
        "**Prices are per unit, and the unit comes after.** *Bi euro kiloa* = \"two euros a kilo\". Note the order: the price first, then the unit with the article — just as English says \"two euros a kilo\".",
        "**Erdi means half, and it goes after.** *Kilo erdi* (half a kilo), *ordu erdi* (half an hour), *bi euro eta erdi* (two and a half euros). Don't confuse it with *erdian*, which means \"in the middle\".",
        "**Garestiagoa, onena — comparisons are glued on too.** Like English *-er* and *-est*, Basque uses endings for \"more\" and \"most\" — but for every adjective, however long. *Garesti* → *garesti**ago**a* (more expensive) → *garesti**en**a* (most expensive). The grammar section below covers it fully."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Colors and describing things",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Euskara",
            "Say it",
            "English",
            "Note"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "zuria",
              "SOO-ree-ah",
              "white",
              "ardo zuria = white wine"
            ],
            [
              "beltza",
              "BEHL-tsah",
              "black",
              "ardo beltza = red wine!"
            ],
            [
              "gorria",
              "GOH-rree-ah",
              "red",
              "ikurrina gorria da"
            ],
            [
              "urdina",
              "oor-DEE-nah",
              "blue",
              "also used for sea green"
            ],
            [
              "berdea",
              "behr-DEH-ah",
              "green",
              ""
            ],
            [
              "horia",
              "OH-ree-ah",
              "yellow",
              "don't confuse it with hori (that)"
            ],
            [
              "marroia",
              "mah-RROH-ee-ah",
              "brown",
              ""
            ],
            [
              "grisa",
              "GREE-sah",
              "gray",
              ""
            ],
            [
              "laranja",
              "lah-RAHN-jah",
              "orange",
              "also the fruit"
            ],
            [
              "arrosa",
              "ah-RROH-sah",
              "pink",
              "also means \"rose\""
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Watch out for one cultural trap: red wine is *ardo **beltza*** — \"black wine\", not red. Basque uses *beltz* for any deep, dark color, and red wine falls into that group. If you order *ardo gorria*, the waiter will understand, but will know at once that you're just learning."
        },
        {
          "type": "kotak",
          "title": "Ikurrina — the Basque flag",
          "items": [
            "It has three colors: a *gorria* (red) background, a *berdea* (green) diagonal cross, and a *zuria* (white) upright cross. Its name is *ikurrina*, from *ikur* (\"symbol\").",
            "The word *gorri* also appears in many place names: *Mendigorria* (\"red mountain\"), *Iturgorri* (\"red spring\").",
            "Colors work like any other adjective, so they go after the noun and carry the article: *kotxe **gorria*** (the red car), *etxe **zuriak*** (the white houses)."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Comparing: -ago, baino, -en, -egi",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Form",
            "Ending",
            "Example",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "basic",
              "—",
              "garestia",
              "expensive"
            ],
            [
              "more",
              "**-ago**",
              "garesti**ago**a",
              "more expensive"
            ],
            [
              "most",
              "**-en**",
              "garesti**en**a",
              "most expensive"
            ],
            [
              "too",
              "**-egi**",
              "garesti**egi**a",
              "too expensive"
            ],
            [
              "as … as",
              "**bezain**",
              "zu **bezain** garestia",
              "as expensive as you"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "To name what you're comparing with, use *baino* (\"than\") — and it goes **before** the adjective, the reverse of English."
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Euskara",
            "Literally",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Gazta ogia **baino** garestiagoa da.",
              "cheese bread-than expensive-more-the is",
              "Cheese is more expensive than bread."
            ],
            [
              "Bilbo Donostia **baino** handiagoa da.",
              "Bilbo Donostia-than big-more-the is",
              "Bilbo is bigger than Donostia."
            ],
            [
              "Hau da onena.",
              "this is good-most-the",
              "This is the best one."
            ],
            [
              "Kafe hau beroegia dago.",
              "coffee this hot-too-the is(located)",
              "This coffee is too hot."
            ],
            [
              "Ni zu bezain altua naiz.",
              "I you as tall-the am",
              "I'm as tall as you."
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "kotak",
          "title": "Three irregular forms to memorize",
          "items": [
            "*on* (good) → **hobea** (better) → **onena** (best).",
            "*txar* (bad) → **okerragoa** (worse) → **txarrena** (worst).",
            "*asko* (a lot) → **gehiago** (more) → **gehiena** (most); *gutxi* (a little) → **gutxiago** → **gutxiena**.",
            "Everything else is completely regular — just stick *-ago*, *-en*, or *-egi* onto the base word before the article."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Shopping phrases and units",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Euskara",
            "Say it",
            "English"
